= Solution
{parent=a}

Write the <power-law size distribution> as $n(D)=kD^{-\alpha}$. The total geometric cross-section of the optically thin <debris disk> is
$$
\sigma_{\rm tot}=\int_{D_{\min}}^{D_{\max}}
\frac{\pi D^2}{4}kD^{-\alpha}\,dD
\simeq\frac{\pi k}{4(\alpha-3)}D_{\min}^{3-\alpha}.
$$
Because blackbody grains at radius $r$ intercept the fraction $\sigma_{\rm tot}/(4\pi r^2)$ of the stellar luminosity, the <fractional luminosity of a debris disk> gives
$$
k=16r^2f(\alpha-3)D_{\min}^{\alpha-3}.
$$
Another <integral> then yields
$$
\boxed{n(>D)=\frac{16r^2f(\alpha-3)}{\alpha-1}
D_{\min}^{\alpha-3}D^{1-\alpha}},
$$
for $D_{\min}\ll D\ll D_{\max}$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For equal material densities, a projectile of diameter $D_{\rm cc}$ catastrophically disperses a target of diameter $D$ when
$$
\frac12\left(\frac{D_{\rm cc}}D\right)^3v_{\rm rel}^2\simeq Q_D^*,
\qquad
D_{\rm cc}=D\left(\frac{2Q_D^*}{v_{\rm rel}^2}\right)^{1/3}.
$$
Assume $D_{\min}\ll D_{\rm cc}\ll D$, neglect gravitational focusing, and approximate the collision cross-section by $\pi D^2/4$. The collision rate per target is
$$
R_{\rm cc}(D)=\frac{\pi D^2}{4}\frac{v_{\rm rel}}Vn(>D_{\rm cc}).
$$
Substitution gives
$$
\boxed{R_{\rm cc}(D)=A
v_{\rm rel}^{(1+2\alpha)/3}
(Q_D^*)^{(1-\alpha)/3}D^{3-\alpha}},
$$
where
$$
\boxed{A=\frac{4\pi r^2f}{V}\frac{\alpha-3}{\alpha-1}
D_{\min}^{\alpha-3}2^{(1-\alpha)/3}}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For size-independent $Q_D^*$, the total rate of destructive events whose target exceeds $D$ is
$$
\mathcal R(>D)=\int_D^{D_{\max}}n(D')R_{\rm cc}(D')\,dD'.
$$
Since the integrand is proportional to $D'^{3-2\alpha}$ and $\alpha>3$, the lower limit dominates. With $n(D)=kD^{-\alpha}$,
$$
\mathcal R(>D)\simeq
\frac{kA}{2\alpha-4}
v_{\rm rel}^{(1+2\alpha)/3}
(Q_D^*)^{(1-\alpha)/3}D^{4-2\alpha}.
$$
The mean interval between such events is therefore
$$
\boxed{t_m(>D)\simeq
\frac{2\alpha-4}{kA}
v_{\rm rel}^{-(1+2\alpha)/3}
(Q_D^*)^{(\alpha-1)/3}D^{2\alpha-4}}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

If $Q_D^*\propto D^b$, part (b) gives
$$
R_{\rm cc}(D)\propto
D^{3-\alpha+b(1-\alpha)/3}.
$$
The mass in one logarithmic size interval scales as $D^3[D n(D)]\propto D^{4-\alpha}$. A steady <collisional cascade> requires the mass destroyed per unit time, and hence the mass flux through every logarithmic interval, to be independent of $D$. Its exponent is therefore
$$
4-\alpha+3-\alpha+\frac{b(1-\alpha)}3=0.
$$
Solving gives
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac{21+b}{6+b}}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= e
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The total destructive-event rate above $D$ scales as
$$
\mathcal R(>D)\propto
D^{4-2\alpha+b(1-\alpha)/3}.
$$
The steady-state relation from part (d) implies
$$
\frac{b(1-\alpha)}3=2\alpha-7,
$$
so the exponent is exactly $-3$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{t_m(>D)=\mathcal R(>D)^{-1}\propto D^3}.
$$
The power $3$ is independent of the disruption-law index $b$: changing $b$ changes the equilibrium size-distribution index $\alpha$ in precisely the compensating way.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= f
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Let the shattering threshold be $Q_s=Q_aD^{-a}$ and the full <catastrophic disruption threshold> be $Q_D^*$. From part (b), the rate of impacts exceeding an energy threshold $Q$ is proportional to $Q^{(1-\alpha)/3}$. The expected number of shattering impacts during one catastrophic-disruption waiting time is therefore
$$
\frac{R_s}{R_{\rm cc}}
=\left(\frac{Q_D^*}{Q_s}\right)^{(\alpha-1)/3}.
$$
Excluding the final catastrophic event, the number of <rubblising collisions> is approximately
$$
\boxed{N_{\rm rub}\simeq
\left(\frac{Q_D^*}{Q_aD^{-a}}\right)^{(\alpha-1)/3}-1}.
$$
For the expression printed in the paper, if the gravity-regime term $Q_bD^{-b}$ dominates, this becomes
$$
\boxed{N_{\rm rub}\simeq
\left(\frac{Q_b}{Q_a}D^{a-b}\right)^{(\alpha-1)/3}-1}.
$$
This estimate assumes independent impacts drawn from the same projectile distribution and ignores structural evolution after each rubblising collision.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

